1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,857
Well, my group has been doing a project on how household waste is recycled

2
00:00:05,857 --> 00:00:06,380
in Britain.

3
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:11,824
We were quite shocked to discover that only 9% of people here in the

4
00:00:11,824 --> 00:00:15,500
UK make an effort to recycle their household waste.

5
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:19,611
This is a lower figure than in most other European countries,

6
00:00:19,611 --> 00:00:24,425
and needs to increase dramatically in the next few years if the government is

7
00:00:24,425 --> 00:00:26,390
going to meet its recycling targets.

8
00:00:26,390 --> 00:00:32,692
The agreed targets for the UK mean that by 2008 we must reduce our

9
00:00:32,692 --> 00:00:37,910
carbon dioxide emissions by 12.5%, compared with 1990.

10
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:43,778
And recycling can help to achieve that goal, in two main ways:

11
00:00:43,778 --> 00:00:50,087
the production of recycled glass and paper uses much less energy than producing them

12
00:00:50,087 --> 00:00:51,489
from virgin materials,

13
00:00:51,489 --> 00:00:58,940
and also recycling reduces greenhouse gas emissions from landfill sites and incineration plants.

14
00:00:58,940 --> 00:01:04,195
As part of our project, we carried out a survey of people in the

15
00:01:04,195 --> 00:01:04,536
street,

16
00:01:04,536 --> 00:01:09,571
and the thing that came up over and over again is that people don't

17
00:01:09,571 --> 00:01:12,700
think it's easy enough to recycle their waste.

18
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:16,515
One problem is that there aren't enough 'drop-off' sites,

19
00:01:16,515 --> 00:01:20,370
that is, the places where the public are supposed to take their waste.

20
00:01:20,370 --> 00:01:28,976
We also discovered that waste that's collected from householders is taken to places called 'bring banks', for sorting

21
00:01:28,976 --> 00:01:30,200
and baling into loads.

22
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:35,436
One problem here is taking out everything that shouldn't have been placed in the

23
00:01:35,436 --> 00:01:36,760
recycling containers:

24
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:41,795
people put all sorts of things into bottle banks, like plastic bags

25
00:01:41,795 --> 00:01:43,340
and even broken umbrellas.

26
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:46,250
All this has to be removed by hand.

27
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:52,652
Another difficulty is that toughened glass used for cooking doesn't fully melt at the

28
00:01:52,652 --> 00:01:54,418
temperature required for other glass,

29
00:01:54,418 --> 00:01:57,770
and so that also has to be picked out by hand.

30
00:01:57,770 --> 00:02:02,451
Glass is easy to recycle because it can be reused over

31
00:02:02,451 --> 00:02:04,560
and over again without becoming weaker.

32
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:08,950
Two million tons of glass is thrown away each year,

33
00:02:08,950 --> 00:02:12,899
that is, seven billion bottles and jars;

34
00:02:12,899 --> 00:02:22,340
but only 500,000 tons of that is collected and recycled.

35
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:26,857
Oddly enough, half the glass that's collected is green,

36
00:02:26,857 --> 00:02:29,146
and a lot of that is imported,

37
00:02:29,146 --> 00:02:32,800
so more green glass is recycled than the UK needs.

38
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:42,434
As a result, new uses are being developed for recycled glass, particularly green glass, for example in fibreglass manufacture

39
00:02:42,434 --> 00:02:44,220
and water filtration.

40
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:49,198
A company called CLF Aggregates makes a product for roads,

41
00:02:49,198 --> 00:02:52,310
and 30% of the material is crushed glass.

42
00:02:52,310 --> 00:03:01,570
For recycling paper, Britain comes second in Europe with 40%, behind Germany's amazing 70%.

43
00:03:01,570 --> 00:03:06,125
When recycling started, there were quality problems,

44
00:03:06,125 --> 00:03:10,520
so it was difficult to use recycled paper in office printers.

45
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:12,545
But these problems have now been solved,

46
00:03:12,545 --> 00:03:18,782
and Martin's, based in South London, produces a range of office stationery which is

47
00:03:18,782 --> 00:03:25,880
100% recycled, costs the same as normal paper and is of equally high quality.

48
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:31,186
But this high quality comes at a cost in terms of the waste produced

49
00:03:31,186 --> 00:03:32,030
during the process.

50
00:03:32,030 --> 00:03:36,428
Over a third of the waste paper that comes in can't be used in

51
00:03:36,428 --> 00:03:39,500
the recycled paper, leaving the question of what to do with it.

52
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:48,397
One firm, Papersave, currently sells this to farmers as a soil conditioner, though this practice will soon be banned

53
00:03:48,397 --> 00:03:50,641
because of transport costs and the smell,

54
00:03:50,641 --> 00:03:55,130
and the company is looking into the possibility of alternative uses.

55
00:03:55,130 --> 00:03:58,444
Plastic causes problems,

56
00:03:58,444 --> 00:04:01,678
because there are so many different types of plastic in use today,

57
00:04:01,678 --> 00:04:04,330
and each one has to be dealt with differently.

58
00:04:04,330 --> 00:04:09,364
Pacrite recycles all sorts of things, from bottles to car bumpers,

59
00:04:09,364 --> 00:04:15,382
and one of its most successful activities is recycling plastic bottles to make containers

60
00:04:15,382 --> 00:04:18,330
which are used all over the country to collect waste.

61
00:04:18,330 --> 00:04:24,746
The Save-a-Cup scheme was set up by the vending and plastics industries to recycle

62
00:04:24,746 --> 00:04:30,660
as many as possible of the three-and-a-half billion polystyrene cups used each year.

63
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:37,515
At the moment 500 million polycups are collected, processed and sold on to other

64
00:04:37,515 --> 00:04:39,639
businesses, such as Waterford,

65
00:04:39,639 --> 00:04:41,544
which turns the cups into pencils,

66
00:04:41,544 --> 00:04:44,410
and Johnson & Jones, a Welsh-based firm,

67
00:04:44,410 --> 00:04:49,080
which has developed a wide variety of items, including business cards.

68
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:55,557
Well, to sum up, there seems to be plenty of research going on into

69
00:04:55,557 --> 00:04:57,402
how to re-use materials,

70
00:04:57,402 --> 00:05:03,458
but the biggest problem is getting people to think about recycling instead of throwing

71
00:05:03,458 --> 00:05:04,040
things away.

72
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:08,550
At least doing the research made us much more careful.
